# Dockerfile.superset — revised
#
# WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY
#
#  1. The psycopg2 install no longer names a Python version.
#
#     Was:  pip install --target=/app/.venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages psycopg2-binary
#
#     `python3.10` is not a path you control — it is whatever Python the base
#     image was built with, and `apache/superset:latest` moves. If the image is
#     3.11 or 3.12, pip creates that python3.10 directory anyway and installs
#     into it, silently, somewhere the interpreter never looks. The install
#     "succeeds"; the driver is then either absent (ModuleNotFoundError at the
#     first query) or coming from somewhere else entirely. Both are worse than
#     a build that fails.
#
#     This version installs with the venv's own interpreter and then PROVES the
#     import in the same layer, so a driver that lands in the wrong place fails
#     the build instead of a database connection three weeks later.
#
#  2. It is worth checking whether this line is needed at all. The official
#     image installs the postgres extra itself (`.[postgres,mysql,...]` in the
#     upstream Dockerfile), so psycopg2 may already be importable:
#
#         docker compose exec superset python -c \
#           "import sys, psycopg2; print(sys.version.split()[0], psycopg2.__file__)"
#
#     A path under /app/.venv/lib/python3.X/site-packages means the driver is
#     there and this RUN line can be deleted. (If it is a /usr/local/... path,
#     you are importing the system copy — also fine on this image.)
#
#  3. The base image is NOT pinned below, and it should be. `apache/superset:latest`
#     was last pushed to Docker Hub on 2026-09-07, so the next `build` pulls a
#     different Superset than the one running now — and rebuilding at the same
#     time as a config change means debugging two things at once. Find out what
#     you are running and pin it:
#
#         docker compose exec superset superset version
#
#     then put that version in the FROM line (the current release is 6.1.0).
#     The image ID you already have is the honest record of today:
#
#         docker image inspect apache/superset:latest --format '{{.Id}}'
#
#  4. The config COPY and SUPERSET_CONFIG_PATH are unchanged — deliberately.
#     That pair is what has been loading your superset_config.py all along. The
#     revised docker-compose.yml also MOUNTS the same path, and the mount wins,
#     so config edits stop needing a rebuild — but the COPY stays, because an
#     image pushed to a registry must carry its own config.
#
#  5. If you already built and pushed an image from this Dockerfile, the old
#     superset_config.py — the one with the secret key written into it — is
#     inside that image's layers. Rotating SUPERSET_SECRET_KEY is what makes
#     that copy worthless; nothing else you do removes it.
#
#  6. This file does NOT run `superset db upgrade` or `superset init`. Those are
#     not build steps: they talk to the metadata database and belong with the
#     running container. Run them when the database is new, or after upgrading
#     Superset:
#
#         docker compose exec superset superset db upgrade
#         docker compose exec superset superset init
#
#  7. Nothing here is a security boundary — but one habit is worth it: the build
#     context is the whole project folder. Today the only COPY is of
#     superset_config.py, so nothing else can leak into an image. If a
#     `.dockerignore` is ever added, put `node_modules`, `.env`, `storage/`,
#     `dist` and `.git` in it before someone writes `COPY . .` — `.env` is about
#     to hold your secret key.

FROM apache/superset:latest        # TODO: pin to the version `superset version` reports

USER root

# Install into the venv Superset itself runs from, and fail the build if the
# import does not come from there. No pythonX.Y anywhere.
RUN /app/.venv/bin/python -m pip install --no-cache-dir psycopg2-binary \
 && /app/.venv/bin/python -c "import psycopg2, sys; print('psycopg2', psycopg2.__version__, 'for python', sys.version.split()[0], '->', psycopg2.__file__)"
# If the build stops with "No module named pip", this image's venv has no pip.
# Use the uv that the official image installs instead:
#   RUN uv pip install --python /app/.venv/bin/python --no-cache-dir psycopg2-binary

# Copy configuration file and set python path
COPY superset_config.py /app/pythonpath/superset_config.py
ENV SUPERSET_CONFIG_PATH=/app/pythonpath/superset_config.py

USER superset
